## Agent harness

This repo uses an orchestrator / builder / reviewer workflow. Hooks, CI and branch
protection enforce the rules below; this section explains them.

### Roles
- **Orchestrator** (main session): plans, writes the contract first, splits work into
  lanes (at most ~3 in parallel), spawns builders and reviewers, runs the merge gate.
  It is the only agent that may use the `HARNESS_ORCHESTRATOR=1` override.
- **Builder** (one per lane, own git worktree and branch `lane-<x>-<slug>`): implements
  test-first, runs `scripts/verify.sh`, pushes its branch, opens one PR. Its PR body starts
  with `<!-- harness:builder id=<lane-id> -->`.
- **Reviewer** (fresh context, never the builder): reviews the diff for correctness,
  security, test quality and contract adherence; posts **one** review with all findings.
  Its review body contains `<!-- harness:reviewer id=<reviewer-id> -->` (an id other than
  the builder's).
- **Human**: tests the app, reviews PRs, approves `sensitive` changes, owns releases.

### Commands
- `scripts/verify.sh`: everything CI runs. **Must pass before you open a PR.**
- `scripts/verify.sh --mutation`: adds mutation testing on packages changed vs `origin/{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`.
- `scripts/pr-ready.sh <n>`: the merge gate. Exit 0 READY, 1 NOT READY, 3 NEEDS HUMAN.
- `scripts/risk-tier.py`: prints `fast` | `standard` | `sensitive` for changed files (rules in `harness.config.json`).

### Rules
1. **TDD.** Commit the failing test (`test(x): ... (red)`), then the fix (`feat(x): ... (green)`).
2. Coverage ≥ {{COVERAGE_MIN}}%, mutation efficacy ≥ {{MUTATION_MIN}}% on changed packages. Assert behaviour, not "no error".
3. Every bug fix adds a row to `docs/catch-log.md` (which layer caught it, which should have).
4. Synthetic data only. No secrets, real customer or staff data, or personal paths.
5. Conventional commits. Merge commits only: never squash or rebase-merge.

### What builders may not do (enforced by `scripts/hooks/guard-bash.py`)
Merge PRs; push to `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`; force-push (including `--force-with-lease`); push tags;
create releases; run or toggle workflows; change repo settings, secrets or variables; any
`gh api` write. The hook fails closed. If `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}` moved, rebase your branch
locally and push it as a **new** branch (or ask the orchestrator to force-push it).

### Per-PR flow
1. Builder opens the PR (template in `.github/pull_request_template.md`).
2. Orchestrator spawns a reviewer; reviewer posts one review.
3. Builder fixes, then posts **one** comment headed `## Review findings → fixes` mapping each
   finding to its fix (commit sha) or why not, plus the final verify numbers.
4. Orchestrator runs `scripts/pr-ready.sh <n>`: checks green (including {{REQUIRED_CHECKS}}),
   a code-scanning analysis of the current merge commit with 0 alerts on the merge and head refs,
   0 unresolved bot threads, an independent (non-bot) review, the findings → fixes comment after
   the last harness review, and a human approval when the risk tier needs one. It prints the
   head sha the verdict is for.
   - Exit 1 (NOT READY): the builder fixes what the `FAIL` lines name. Any `gh` error is NOT READY.
   - Exit 3 (NEEDS HUMAN): stop and ask the human, naming the PR and its risk tier. Don't merge or
     retry. On a GitHub account shared by the human and the agents, no approval can satisfy the
     gate, so the human merges these PRs themselves.
5. The human (or the orchestrator, if the human allowed it and the gate says READY) merges with a
   merge commit. The orchestrator merges only the judged commit:
   `HARNESS_ORCHESTRATOR=1 gh pr merge <n> --merge --match-head-commit <sha>` (the sha pr-ready
   printed); if GitHub refuses because the head moved, re-run the gate. Remaining lanes rebase on `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`.
